# Implementation Dependency Order

Although GitHub can contain all 10 main issues from the beginning, the implementation dependency chain should effectively be:

```text
Issue 1
Repository + Architecture Foundation
        │
        ▼
Issue 2
Secure Electron Boundary
        │
        ▼
Issue 3
Document State + Storage + Recovery
        │
        ├──────────────┐
        ▼              ▼
Issue 4            Issue 6 partial
Markdown Core      Basic App Shell
        │
        ▼
Issue 5
Four Editing Modes
        │
        ├──────────────────┐
        ▼                  ▼
Issue 6                Issue 7
Workspace/UX           Export System
        │                  │
        └────────┬─────────┘
                 ▼
             Issue 8
       Security + Operations
                 │
                 ▼
             Issue 9
       Windows Distribution
                 │
                 ▼
            Issue 10
     Final Release Qualification
```

The most important rule for the project is that **Issue 10 tests are not postponed until Issue 10**. Every sub-issue above should ship its own tests when implemented. Issue 10 completes cross-system, release-artifact, performance, installer, compatibility, and acceptance testing.

This backlog preserves the core architectural invariants from all 11 documents: **Markdown remains canonical; renderer authority is restricted; files are accessed through capabilities; saves are revision-aware and atomic; external changes cannot be silently overwritten; recovery is separate from Save; unsupported syntax is preserved; expensive operations are cancellable/isolated; exports use immutable revision snapshots and shared IR; Windows 11 is first-class; deployment artifacts are signed/tested; and security/data-loss regressions block release.**

